  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: author.name,
    section: "Plant Protein",
    tags: [
      "best pumpkin seed protein powder",
      "pumpkin seed protein",
      "pumpkin protein powder",
      "pumpkin seed protein powder organic"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker={"Buyer's Guide"} badge={"Label-Checked"} author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">Best Pumpkin Seed Protein Powder</Fragment>
      Six pumpkin seed protein powders compared on what the label really delivers per gram, with an honest look at lysine, minerals, the green color and what 25 g of protein costs.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#what-it-is">What It Is</a>
      <a href="#per-scoop">Protein per Scoop</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">Our Top Picks</a>
      <a href="#amino-acids">Amino Acids</a>
      <a href="#minerals-fiber">Fiber and Minerals</a>
      <a href="#allergen-friendly">Who It Is For</a>
      <a href="#taste-color">Taste and Color</a>
      <a href="#heavy-metals">Heavy Metals</a>
      <a href="#cost-per-25g">Cost per 25 g</a>
      <a href="#how-to-use">How to Use It</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          Pumpkin seed protein is the plant protein for people who cannot or will not use pea, soy or dairy. It is a pressed, milled seed powder with one ingredient, about 55 to 67 percent protein by weight, and more magnesium, iron and zinc per scoop than almost any other powder. It also has real limits: it is low in lysine, it costs more than pea, it turns your shake green, and a scoop often gives 10 to 20 g of protein rather than 25. Here is how to choose one, and when a blend makes more sense.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="what-it-is" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. What Pumpkin Seed Protein Actually Is
          </h2>

          <p>
            Pumpkin seed protein is made from hulled pumpkin seeds, the green pepitas you see in trail mix. The seeds are pressed to remove most of their oil, and the pressed cake that is left is milled and sifted into a fine powder. Most brands describe the process as cold-pressed or simply pressed, with no solvent extraction. The result is a single-ingredient food that is mostly protein, with a little residual fat, some fiber and a meaningful dose of minerals.
          </p>

          <p>
            That puts it in the same family as <a href="/best-hemp-protein-powder/">hemp protein</a>: a minimally processed seed powder rather than a refined isolate. The difference is concentration. Typical hemp powders land near 50 percent protein by weight, while the pumpkin seed powders in this guide run from about 55 to 67 percent, per their labels. That is less than a pea isolate, which is usually 80 percent or more, but enough to make pumpkin seed a usable protein powder rather than just a smoothie booster.
          </p>

          <p>
            The labels tell you very little about how a brand gets there. Some sift more of the fiber out, which raises protein per gram; others leave more of the seed intact, which raises fat, fiber and calories. That is why two powders made from the same seed can list 10 g of protein in a 15 g serving and 18 g in a 30 g serving. Neither is wrong. They are slightly different products, and the only fair way to compare them is protein per gram of powder.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pumpkin seed protein is rarely the headline ingredient in mainstream shakes. When it appears on a big-brand label, it is usually as a supporting protein in a pea-led blend, as in Truvani, OWYN and several greens-and-protein products. Single-ingredient pumpkin protein is a smaller niche, sold mostly by organic specialty brands. That is part of why the choice is narrow and prices vary so widely between bags that look almost identical.
          </p>

          <p>
            This guide sits alongside our guides to <a href="/best-pea-protein-powder/">pea protein</a>, <a href="/best-brown-rice-protein-powder/">brown rice protein</a> and hemp. If you are choosing between the plant proteins rather than between pumpkin brands, read the comparison sections below first, then check our <a href="/best-plant-based-protein-powder/">plant-based protein powder</a> roundup for the finished blends that combine several of them.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="per-scoop" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Protein per Scoop: Why the Labels Look So Different
          </h2>

          <p>
            Pumpkin seed protein labels are hard to compare at a glance because serving sizes range from 15 g to 36 g. NOW lists 10 g of protein in a 15 g serving, Micro Ingredients 18 g in about 30 g, Probase 19 g in 30 g, and Sprout Living 20 g in 32.5 g for the unflavored version and 20 g in 36 g for vanilla, all per the labels at the time of writing. On the front of the jar, NOW looks like the weakest. Per gram, it is the strongest.
          </p>

          <p>
            The table below converts every label to the same question: how much powder, and how many calories, does it take to get 25 g of protein? This is the number to use when you compare pumpkin seed with whey or pea, and when you plan a shake. A typical pea isolate needs about 30 g of powder for 25 g of protein. Most pumpkin seed powders need about 38 to 45 g.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Powder</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Protein per serving (label)</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Powder for 25 g protein</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Calories at 25 g protein</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">NOW Sports Pumpkin Seed</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">10 g per 15 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 38 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 125</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Probase Nutrition</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">19 g per 30 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 40 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 130</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sprout Living (unflavored)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">20 g per 32.5 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 41 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 190</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Micro Ingredients</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">18 g per 30 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 42 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 165</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sprout Living (vanilla)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">20 g per 36 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 45 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 210</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Truvani blend (chocolate)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">20 g per 33 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 41 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 165</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Calories vary more than protein does. The gap comes mostly from fat. Sprout Living lists 5 g of fat per serving, which is real pumpkin seed oil left in the powder, while NOW and Probase list 0 g. If you are using protein powder to cut calories, that difference adds up to about 60 calories per 25 g of protein. If you are using it in smoothies or oats, the extra fat is not a problem, and some of it is the omega-6 and other fats that pumpkin seeds are known for.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical upshot is simple. Do not compare pumpkin seed scoops with whey scoops. Decide how much protein you want from the shake, which our guide to <a href="/how-much-protein-do-i-need/">how much protein you need</a> can help with, then weigh or measure the powder to hit it. For most people that means two scoops of Sprout Living or Micro Ingredients, or three of NOW's small tablespoons, to land near 25 g.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The Best Pumpkin Seed Protein Powders, Ranked
          </h2>

          <p>
            We ranked these on protein per gram of powder, ingredient simplicity, minerals, the testing each brand documents, how widely they are sold and approximate cost per 25 g of protein. Five are pumpkin-only powders; one is a pea-led blend that includes pumpkin seed. Label figures are per the label or retailer listing at the time of writing and vary by flavor and batch. We did not taste-test these; taste and mixing notes come from buyer feedback.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
