        <div id="how-to-use" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. How to Use Pumpkin Seed Protein
          </h2>

          <p>
            Use it by grams of protein, not scoops. Decide how much protein you want from the shake or meal, then measure the powder. About 38 to 45 g of most pumpkin powders gives 25 g of protein. A kitchen scale makes this easy the first few times, and after that you will know roughly how many scoops it takes.
          </p>

          <p>
            Blend rather than shake. Pumpkin seed protein mixes better in a blender with fruit, nut butter or oats than in a shaker bottle with water. If you do use a shaker, add the liquid first, then the powder, and let it sit for a minute before shaking. Plant milks such as oat or soy improve the texture, and soy milk adds lysine, which helps the amino acid balance.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pair it with a lysine source, or mix it with pea. A simple half-and-half mix of pumpkin and unflavored pea protein gives you a more balanced amino acid profile and brings the cost per 25 g down. If you want greens in the same scoop, our batch guide to the <a href="/best-greens-protein-powder/">best greens protein powder</a> covers blends that already combine plant protein with vegetables, some of which include pumpkin seed.
          </p>

          <p>
            Store it cool and closed. Pumpkin seed protein contains some residual oil, especially the higher-fat powders, so it can go stale faster than an isolate once opened. Keep the bag sealed, away from heat and light, and use it within a few months of opening. If it smells sharp or paint-like, the fat has gone rancid and it is time to replace it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing scoops instead of grams of protein</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                NOW lists 10 g of protein per serving and Sprout Living lists 20 g, but NOW's serving is less than half the size. Per gram of powder, NOW is actually more concentrated. Always compare protein per gram, or the powder needed for 25 g, before deciding that one pumpkin powder is weaker than another.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Making pumpkin seed your only protein</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Pumpkin seed protein is low in lysine, so relying on it alone for most of your daily protein is not ideal. Pair it with legumes, soy, dairy or eggs elsewhere in your day, or use a pea and pumpkin blend, so the amino acid profile balances out across meals.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Ignoring the facility allergen statement</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A one-ingredient label does not mean an allergen-free facility. Several pumpkin seed brands are packed alongside dairy, soy, wheat, peanuts or tree nuts. If you have a true allergy rather than a preference, ask the brand about cross-contact controls before buying and follow your allergist's advice.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Expecting it to taste like whey</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Unflavored pumpkin seed protein is green, earthy and nutty. In plain water most buyers find it hard to enjoy. Plan to use it in smoothies, oats or baking, or buy a flavored version such as Sprout Living vanilla, rather than giving up after one unpleasant shaker bottle.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Taking three servings a day of one seed powder</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Plant proteins can carry trace lead and cadmium, and few pumpkin brands publish their numbers. Using one seed powder heavily every day concentrates any exposure. Rotate between pumpkin, pea and other proteins, and ask brands for a certificate of analysis if you rely on one powder.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="faq-list">
            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is pumpkin seed protein a complete protein?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  It contains all nine essential amino acids, so it is technically complete. But lysine is low relative to human needs, which makes it the limiting amino acid. That is fine if you eat legumes, soy, dairy or eggs elsewhere. If pumpkin seed is your main protein, a pea and pumpkin blend gives a more balanced profile.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>How much protein is in pumpkin seed protein powder?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Per the labels at the time of writing, the powders here give 10 to 20 g of protein per serving, but serving sizes run from 15 g to 36 g. By weight, they are about 55 to 67 percent protein. Expect to use roughly 38 to 45 g of powder for 25 g of protein, compared with about 30 g for pea isolate.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is pumpkin seed protein better than pea protein?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Not overall. Pea protein is cheaper, denser and richer in lysine. Pumpkin seed wins on minerals such as magnesium, iron and zinc, and on allergen profile, since it is not a legume. Many people do best with both, either in a blend or by alternating, which also balances their amino acids nicely.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Why is my pumpkin seed protein green?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Hulled pumpkin seeds are naturally green, and pressing and milling them keeps that color. It is normal and not a sign of added greens or spoilage. In chocolate or berry smoothies the color mostly disappears. If the powder smells sharp or paint-like, though, its residual oil may have gone rancid and it should be replaced.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Does pumpkin seed protein contain heavy metals?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  Like all plant proteins, it can contain trace lead and cadmium from soil. Few brands publish results, and one listing here carries a Prop 65 lead warning, which reflects a very low threshold. Choose brands that share test results, rotate protein sources and avoid relying on one seed powder at several servings a day.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is pumpkin seed protein good for building muscle?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  It can support muscle gain when total daily protein is adequate, but it is not the most efficient option. It is lower in leucine and lysine than whey. Aim for about 25 to 30 g of protein per shake, and combine it with pea or other lysine-rich foods. Training and total intake matter far more than the specific powder.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

            <details class="faq-item">
              <summary><h3>Is pumpkin seed protein safe for everyone?</h3></summary>
              <div class="faq-answer">
                <p>
                  For most healthy adults, a serving or two a day is a reasonable food. People with seed allergies, kidney disease, iron overload conditions, pregnancy or regular medication should check with a doctor or registered dietitian first, especially because these powders are rich in minerals. This guide is general information and not medical advice.
                </p>
              </div>
            </details>

          </div>
        </div>

